On the warped last 5 mm, I think that's a separate thing, and probably not the picture. `--r-max` only decides how far out the drawing goes; it doesn't change where anything lands. But `geo` by default measures only up to about 85 % of the recorded area, around 54 mm, so beyond that the geometry is extrapolated. With `--r-max 58` you drew about 4 mm further out than was measured, and that's where it went wrong. We've seen the outer edge drift on another drive too. You can check how far your measurement reached: `measured_span_mm` in `results/geo-E-20261107-192227/summary.json`. To cover the outer part, measure the random disc again with a range reaching near its end, for example `--ranges 70000-2290000`, and if `geo` then suggests a correction file, use it.

@LTVA1 You're right that this isn't calibration. The `info` shows what happened: the second disc stopped at LBA 1376672, about 47.5 mm, and everything past that is unwritten, which is your empty last centimetre. Sorry, our script's message ("the data has probably reached the disc and closing failed") was wrong here. It guesses from the elapsed time, and the drive was simply slower than the estimate. I'll change it to ask the drive how far it actually got.

It was much slower: 1376672 sectors in 16 minutes is about 1400 sectors a second, around half of 4x. Drives do that when writing gets hard: they retune the laser power and drop speed, and those changes can leave rings that look different, which may be your warped rings. So I read the second disc as the drive struggling with the outer half and finally giving up, more than as a picture landing in the wrong place. The first disc got through, but its last 5 mm may have been the same struggle.

What I can't separate yet is why: these discs, the drive in its USB case (a slim drive draws the most power while writing, and some USB cases can't quite give it, so a case with its own power supply or a Y-cable can help), or our data. Since DiscImageStudio writes to 58 mm fine with the same drive and discs, our data is still a suspect. Our image here also runs to the very last sector (2295104, the whole disc). If you try again, change one thing at a time; `--r-max 56` would be the cheapest first step.

